<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Via Egnatia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 146–120 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Via <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egnatia">Egnatia</a> was a Roman road built in the later 2nd century BCE by the proconsul Gnaeus Egnatius after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-roman-province">Macedonia</a> became a province. It ran about 1,100 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Dyrrachium</a> on the Adriatic across the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a> and Macedonia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>, and was later extended to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. It joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appian-way">Via Appia</a> across the Adriatic to link <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> with the east and remained a major route through the Byzantine period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/via-egnatia.jpg" alt="Via Egnatia" width="320" height="262" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eric Gaba (Sting - fr:Sting) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Via_Egnatia-en.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
